US-030 is implemented on Vulkan, and every Windows and Linux quality gate passes except the native Linux Vulkan GPU run, which this WSL host can't execute. The Windows RTX 5080 Vulkan GPU suite passes with no validation errors.

**What was built.** Before this story every Vulkan resource and descriptor call returned `Unsupported`, so this was implementation rather than verification.
- **Staging and readback:** `CpuWrite` upload buffers are host-visible, coherent and persistently mapped. `upload_buffer` and `copy_buffer_to_texture` follow the same rules as the DX12 side.
- **Synchronization2:** all barriers and submissions go through the sync2 entry points. The extension and its feature are required at device creation.
- **Descriptors:** layouts, pool pages, set writes, and dispatch-time state checks. `ShaderResource` and `UnorderedAccess` bindings are `VK_DESCRIPTOR_TYPE_MUTABLE_EXT`, so a binding can hold a buffer or an image as the RHI contract allows.
- **Compute pipelines:** adapter per-stage limits are checked before any Vulkan pipeline object is created.
- **Queue waits and accounting:** `queue_wait` is carried by the next submission on that queue. `resource_stats()` tracks live sets, and a destroyed set frees at its fence.

**Choices I made that you should know about:**
- **Dedicated allocations, not VMA.** Each resource gets its own allocation, matching DX12's committed resources. The vendored VMA is not wired in, so suballocation is not implemented.
- **Mutable descriptors.** This needs `VK_EXT_mutable_descriptor_type`. Without it, shader-resource and UAV layouts fail with `Unsupported`, and constant-buffer layouts still work.
- **Graphics pipelines, draws, and mesh pipelines** still return `Unsupported`, naming US-031 and US-038.

**Results:**
- **Linux:** Clang and GCC `ValidateVendor`, presets, and unit suites pass 22/22. ASan/UBSan `linux-sanitize` passes with no reports.
- **Windows:** `ValidateVendor`, `win-dev`, `win-debug` (0 MSVC warnings) and `win-unit` 21/21 all pass.
- **RTX 5080:** `--suite gpu --backend vulkan` passes 14 cases and 520 assertions with no validation errors. The DX12 cross-check passes 13 cases and 508 assertions.
- **Not run:** the native Linux Vulkan GPU suite (this host has no usable Vulkan device or validation layer; it exits 3 `unsupported`), GitHub CI (not pushed), the acceptance benchmark (no bench target exists yet), and the GUI check.

**Process:** the first Windows GPU run failed on two real validation errors. A barrier used vertex and fragment stages on the compute and copy queues, and a copy-only texture got an image view it can't have. A submitted command list also kept its bound sets alive, which delayed a destroyed set's free past its fence. The Linux builds and tests passed throughout, because WSL has no Vulkan device, so only the RTX run caught these.

I set `passes: true` for US-030 in `tasks/prd.json`, following US-027 and US-029, which were set true with the same Linux GPU gap. If you'd rather hold the flag until the Linux GPU run exists, it's a one-line change.

I did not commit anything, per the workflow. The progress log, `docs/vulkan.md`, `docs/rhi.md` and `docs/build.md` are updated.
